\"quote\": \"DESTRUCTIVE = (\\\"reset --hard\\\", \\\"branch -D\\\", \\\"push --force\\\")\"},\n      \"falsification\": {\"counter\": \"'push --force' substring could match 'clean -fd'\", \"survived\": true, \"why\": \"no overlapping substring between 'push --force' and 'clean -fd'\"}\n    }\n  ],\n  \"dropped\": [\n    {\n      \"candidate\": \"Naive substring match on joined argv could over-block unrelated commands containing e.g. 'stash' as part of a longer token.\",\n      \"counter\": \"contract states only which commands must be refused/allowed, not precision of matching strategy\",\n      \"why_it_died\": \"not a verifiable contract violation, only a speculative false-positive risk\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"halt\": null\n}\n```","exit":0,"model":"claude-sonnet-5","input_tokens":54447,"output_tokens":2592,"wall_ms":30970,"tool_calls":0,"valid":true}
+{"case":"guard","arm":"bmad","run":1,"result":"- Substring matching on joined argv is trivially bypassable: `git reset  --hard` (double space), `git reset --har` won't match but `git rst --hard` alias would, and unrelated commands containing the substring incidentally (e.g., a commit message argument `\"please reset --hard later\"`) would be wrongly blocked — the check has no notion of actual git subcommand/flag parsing.\n- Contract requires blocking `checkout -- .`, `restore --worktree`, `clean -fd`, but `DESTRUCTIVE` tuple only contains `reset --hard`, `branch -D`, `push --force` — three of six required patterns are simply missing from implementation.\n- `stash` is required to be blocked on a dirty worktree per contract, but `\"stash\"` is absent from `DESTRUCTIVE`, so `git stash` would be allowed even though it can destroy uncommitted changes in some workflows (or at minimum contradicts explicit spec).\n- No test file/fixture accompanies `guard.py` in this bundle — the eval fixture directory implies tests should verify `allowed()`, but none are shown; can't confirm behavior against contract cases.\n- `push --force` matches command string, but real-world usage often uses `--force-with-lease` or short flag `-f`; neither is covered, meaning force-pushes can bypass the guard entirely.\n- `branch -D` won't match `git branch -d -D` variations or `-D` combined with other flags/ordering (e.g., `git branch --force -D`), and case-sensitivity isn't addressed (`--Force`, `RESET --HARD` on case-insensitive shells).\n- The `dirty` parameter is a caller-supplied boolean with no validation or documentation on how it's computed — if the caller mis-detects a dirty worktree (e.g., untracked-only changes, ignored files), the guard silently allows destructive commands with no defense in depth.\n- No handling of compound/chained commands (`git add . && git reset --hard`, `; git push --force`) — since `command = \" \".join(argv)` just joins argv, this is somewhat mitigated for a single argv list, but if argv itself is a shell string split naively, quoting/escaping tricks (e.g., inserting the flag as part of a quoted commit message) aren't excluded, risking false positives on unrelated text.\n- On a clean worktree, the contract says \"ordinary non-destructive git commands remain allowed\" but says nothing about whether destructive commands should also be permitted when clean — `guard.py` implicitly allows all six destructive patterns when `dirty=False`, which is unverified against intent (should `push --force` ever be blocked regardless of dirty state, e.g., on protected branches?). The contract is silent/ambiguous here, and the implementation makes an assumption without documenting it.\n- Function name `allowed` and single boolean return give no reason/explanation for a rejection, making it hard for any calling UI/CLI to tell the user *why* the command was blocked or which fragment triggered it.\n- No `__future__`/type-checking on `argv` contents (e.g., non-string elements) — `\" \".join(argv)` will raise `TypeError` on any non-str item with no guard or error handling, an unhandled crash path for malformed input.","exit":0,"model":"claude-sonnet-5","input_tokens":53597,"output_tokens":1073,"wall_ms":53370,"tool_calls":0,"valid":true}
+{"case":"guard","arm":"osmani","run":1,"result":"## Doubt Cycle — guard.py\n\n**Step 1: CLAIM**\nClaim: \"`guard.py` correctly refuses all destructive git operations listed in the contract when the worktree is dirty, and allows everything else.\"\nWhy it matters: this is a safety guard meant to prevent exactly the class of incident described in the user's own CLAUDE.md (accidental `reset --hard` / `checkout -- .` / `stash` on a shared worktree wiping someone else's uncommitted work). A gap here reproduces that incident class silently.\n\n**Step 2: EXTRACT** — artifact and contract as supplied above (no reasoning carried in).\n\n**Step 3: DOUBT (adversarial findings)**\n\n1. **Contract coverage gap (critical):** The contract requires refusing `reset --hard`, `checkout -- .`, `restore --worktree`, `stash`, `clean -fd`, `branch -D`, and `push --force` when dirty. `DESTRUCTIVE` only contains `\"reset --hard\"`, `\"branch -D\"`, `\"push --force\"`. **Four of seven required patterns — `checkout -- .`, `restore --worktree`, `stash`, `clean -fd` — are entirely unguarded.** A dirty-worktree `git stash` or `git clean -fd` passes `allowed()` as `True`.\n2. **Substring matching is both under- and over-inclusive:**\n   - Under-inclusive: equivalent/long-form flags bypass detection entirely — `push -f` (short for `--force`), `branch --delete --force`, `clean -f -d` (split flags instead of `-fd`), `reset --hard HEAD~1` still matches by luck (substring present), but reordered or spaced variants (e.g. extra whitespace collapsing differently, `reset  --hard`) are not normalized before matching.\n   - Over-inclusive: `command = \" \".join(argv)` naively concatenates all argv tokens, so a commit message argument literally containing the text `\"reset --hard\"` (e.g. `git commit -m \"docs: warn about reset --hard\"`) would false-positive and block a harmless commit.\n3. **No token-boundary awareness:** substring checks like `\"branch -D\"` would also match `\"branch -Dxyz\"` or a differently-intended token concatenation, since `argv` is joined with a single space and matched as raw text rather than compared token-by-token.\n4. **Silent fail-open by default:** any command not explicitly enumerated (including the four missing ones above) returns `True` even when dirty — the function's design is an allowlist-by-omission for destructive ops, which is the opposite of a safe default for a guard whose explicit purpose is loss-prevention.\n5. **No case handling / no `git` prefix stripping considered**, though lower risk than the above.\n\n**Step 4: RECONCILE**\n\n- Finding 1 (missing 4 patterns) → **Valid + actionable.** Direct, unambiguous violation of the stated contract text. Must fix: add `\"checkout -- .\"`, `\"restore --worktree\"`, `\"stash\"`, `\"clean -fd\"` to `DESTRUCTIVE` (or restructure matching to be robust to flag variants).\n- Finding 2 (flag-form bypass: `-f` vs `--force`, split flags) → **Valid trade-off / actionable depending on scope.** Contract text only specifies the exact forms listed (`push --force`, not `-f`), so strictly the contract is satisfied for the literal forms — but this is a real security gap for the guard's evident purpose. Flag as a contract ambiguity: recommend tightening the contract or the implementation to cover common aliases.\n- Finding 3 (false-positive on commit messages containing destructive-looking text) → **Valid + actionable.** `\" \".join(argv)` matching raw joined text instead of parsing subcommand + flags is a real correctness bug independent of the contract, since it also risks blocking legitimate non-destructive commands.\n- Finding 4 (fail-open by default) → **Valid + actionable**, same root cause as Finding 1 — restructuring as a matched-pattern allowlist-of-safe-ops or a stricter parser would close both.\n- Finding 5 → **Noise-adjacent / minor**, not contract-required, no action needed now.\n\n**Step 5: STOP**\nFindings are substantive and concentrated in one cycle (not trivial, not repeat). Stop condition: escalate rather than loop — the artifact does not satisfy the contract as written (4/7 required refusals unimplemented) and has an independent false-positive matching bug. **Verdict: FAILS CONTRACT — do not ship as-is.**","exit":0,"model":"claude-sonnet-5","input_tokens":58731,"output_tokens":3154,"wall_ms":39916,"tool_calls":0,"valid":true}
+{"case":"guard","arm":"codex","run":1,"result":"[P1] Guard misses several contract-required destructive commands — guard.py:1\n\nThe `DESTRUCTIVE` tuple only lists `\"reset --hard\"`, `\"branch -D\"`, and `\"push --force\"`. The contract requires blocking six patterns on a dirty worktree: `reset --hard`, `checkout -- .`, `restore --worktree`, `stash`, `clean -fd`, `branch -D`, and `push --force`. `checkout -- .`, `restore --worktree`, `stash`, and `clean -fd` are missing entirely, so `allowed([\"git\", \"checkout\", \"--\", \".\"], dirty=True)`, `allowed([\"git\", \"stash\"], dirty=True)`, and `allowed([\"git\", \"clean\", \"-fd\"], dirty=True)` all incorrectly return `True` on a dirty worktree, letting the guard approve commands the contract says must be refused.\n\nNo findings related to the clean-worktree behavior — that path is correctly permissive for all commands.\n\nOverall assessment: the substring-match design itself is reasonable for the covered cases, but the check fails its core purpose because most of the required destructive-command list isn't in `DESTRUCTIVE`. This should be fixed by adding the missing fragments before this guard is relied upon. No tests are present in the bundle to verify any of the six required refusal cases or the clean-worktree allow case.","exit":0,"model":"claude-sonnet-5","input_tokens":53666,"output_tokens":457,"wall_ms":10791,"tool_calls":0,"valid":true}
